<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Buying vs Renting</span>
<h1>Long-stay Tourists: Rent vs Buy Decision for Hanoi Motorbikes</h1>
<p>The tourist who stays in Hanoi for a season rather than a holiday sits in the interesting middle of the motorbike question, where daily rental starts to sting and ownership starts to tempt, and neither answer is finished without the other beside it. This guide writes the decision for the long-stay visitor specifically, the person whose plans are measured in months and whose calendar is softer than an expat&rsquo;s, and it keeps the arithmetic honest: verified rental figures in ink, ownership categories in pencil, and every live question handed to official sources and a desk with nothing to sell.</p>
<p>The machines, the briefing and the honest numbers come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the desk briefs long-stay visitors on both doors of the question honestly, because the shop rents machines and never sells them and therefore has no favourite answer. Begin at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-for-sale/">motorbike for sale hub</a>, study the long-stay arrangements on the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental</a> page, check the verified figures on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, review the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for maintenance and safety, and keep <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/">renting a motorbike in Vietnam</a> open for the legal layer.</p>
